// RFC-0032 — element invalidation: the retained layout path and a real dirty
// set.
//
// Like the `profiling` example, the demo here is partly the block in your
// terminal — but this one you can also *see*, because the change it makes is
// the difference between a frame that costs 45 ms and one that costs 3 ms on
// the same content.
//
// Run it (from the repo root):
//   cd crates/byard-cli/examples/incremental
//   cargo run -p byard-cli -- dev
//
// ── The one sentence ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Twelve wrapping paragraphs sit under a spinner. The spinner changes a
// rotation angle every frame; the paragraphs change nothing, ever. Before
// RFC-0032 the engine had no way to know that, so every frame tore down the
// layout tree, re-measured every paragraph, re-shaped every glyph and redrew
// the whole window. Now it knows.
//
// ── What to verify, in the terminal ───────────────────────────────────────
//
//   1. `atlas  retained · N node(s) marked` — the new line. It says which
//      path the frame took. In steady state it must read `retained`; a
//      `rebuild` every frame is the thing this example exists to make
//      visible.
//
//   2. `layout.taffy` is ~0.01 ms, not ~0.2 ms. Nothing about the paragraphs
//      changed, so Taffy recomputes nothing and — this is the part that
//      matters — the text measure protocol is never invoked for them.
//
//   3. `encode.glyphs` is ~2 ms, not ~45 ms. This is the same fact one layer
//      down: the paragraphs' `TextLine`s are reported clean, so the glyph
//      cache is not asked to re-shape them.
//
//   4. `present.acquire` is large (~13 ms of a 16.7 ms frame). That is the
//      engine finishing early and waiting for the display — the healthy
//      state. On this same scene before RFC-0032 it was 0.06 ms, because the
//      engine had nothing left over.
//
// ── What to verify, on screen ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   5. THE PARAGRAPHS ARE STILL WRAPPED. This is the whole hazard of the
//      retained path in one line. The incremental layout pass runs the
//      measure protocol, and a version of it without a text sizer would size
//      every wrapping `Text` at its natural *single-line* width — silently
//      un-wrapping every paragraph on the frame after any retained one.
//      If you ever see these paragraphs collapse into long single lines
//      running off the right edge, that is the bug (RFC-0032 §R5).
//
//   6. TAPPING THE COUNTER STILL LANDS ON THE COUNTER. Press "grow" a few
//      times: the top box gets taller and everything below it moves down.
//      Nothing marked those paragraphs dirty — they were pushed by a sibling
//      — and the tap targets move with them anyway, because the spatial grid
//      is rebuilt from resolved rects rather than from anything this RFC
//      computes. An element that looks like it moved but answers taps where
//      it used to be is invisible in a screenshot; this button is how you
//      check.
//
//   7. EDITING TEXT STILL REFLOWS. "longer" swaps the first paragraph for a
//      much longer one. Text content is layout-class, so the paragraph grows
//      and everything below it moves. If text content were misclassified as
//      paint-only, the string would change and the line count would not.
View Main() {
    var count = 0
    var grown = false
    var longer = false

    let paras = ["one", "two", "three", "four", "five", "six",
                 "seven", "eight", "nine", "ten", "eleven", "twelve"]
    let chip = style { radius: 18, p: (vertical: 8, horizontal: 16), color: 0xE6E1E5 }

    Column #[bg: 0x101014, p: 20, gap: 12, width: 760] {
        Text("RFC-0032 — element invalidation") #[color: 0xFFFFFF, size: 24, weight: bold]
        Text("Twelve paragraphs that never change, under one thing that changes every frame.")
            #[color: 0x9AA0AC, size: 13]

        Row #[gap: 20, align: center] {
            // The only thing in the frame that changes on its own. `rotate` is
            // a paint-class attribute, so it never marks layout dirty — which
            // is the invariant RFC-0010 asserted in prose and RFC-0032 turned
            // into a compile error.
            Box #[width: 44, height: 44, origin: center,
                  rotate: 360deg with anim.linear(1400ms, from: 0deg, repeat: infinite)] {
                Canvas #[width: 44, height: 44] {
                    arc(cx: 22, cy: 22, r: 17, stroke: 0x36323C, stroke_width: 4)
                    arc(cx: 22, cy: 22, r: 17, start: -90, sweep: 250,
                        stroke: 0xD0BCFF, stroke_width: 4, cap: round)
                }
            }

            // Grows a box so every sibling below reflows without being marked.
            Button(grown ? "shrink" : "grow") #[..chip, bg: 0x4A4458] => grown = !grown
            Button(longer ? "shorter" : "longer") #[..chip, bg: 0x4A4458] => longer = !longer
            Button("tapped {count}") #[..chip, bg: 0x6750A4] => count++
        }

        // Layout-class: its height is a value, so changing it marks this node
        // and Taffy reflows everything below. Nothing else is marked.
        Box #[width: 200, height: grown ? 110 : 30, radius: 10, bg: 0x2B2930] {}

        Column #[gap: 8] {
            Text(longer
                ? "Text content is layout-class, and this is the row the whole RFC turns on: the string that reaches the frame is hashed alongside the font size and the offered width, so an edit marks this leaf and Taffy re-measures it — and only it. Everything below moves; nothing above is touched; no other paragraph is re-shaped."
                : "Text content is layout-class. Press \"longer\".")
                #[color: 0xCCD2E0, size: 14]
            for p in paras {
                Text("Paragraph {p} — this text never changes. It is measured once, shaped once, and then skipped on every frame after that, which is where the twenty-fold difference on this scene comes from. It wraps, and it must stay wrapped across a retained frame.")
                    #[color: 0x8E8B94, size: 13]
            }
        }
    }
}
